# ===========================================================================
# 15 — Portability as a declaration: `@targets(…)` (ADR-0058)
# ===========================================================================
# Character: portability is not one bit — it is a configurable *set of target
# environments*, selected by need (ADR-0058 §2). A module states the contract it
# promises with `@targets(…)`; the compiler then GATES it: every `pub` function
# must *reach* every listed target (`Rian.Reach`), or compilation fails. A module
# with no `@targets` is not gated — constraints are selected by need, including
# the need for none while prototyping.
#
#   @targets(ex, rs, js)   -> every pub fn must be fully portable
#   @targets(ex)           -> BEAM-only is allowed (host FFI is fine)
#   (no annotation)        -> no gate (pure analysis via `mix rian.targets`)
#
# Reachability propagates along the call graph: a pub function is at most as
# portable as the least-portable function it calls.
#
# ADRs: 0058 (configurable target environments) · 0057 (concurrency native-per-target)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A fully-portable module: pure sequential logic, reaches every target. The
# `@targets(ex, rs, js)` contract holds, so this compiles. `Int53` is the portable
# all-target integer (a native JS `number`, an `i64` elsewhere); a fixed-width
# `Int64` would be off `:js` and break the contract (ADR-0064 §2a).
@targets(ex, rs, js)
mod Geometry do
  pub def square(n Int53) Int53 := n * n

  pub def area_of_rect(w Int53, h Int53) Int53 := w * h
end

# A BEAM-only module: it uses Erlang FFI (`:erlang.system_time/0`), which is
# ex-only by design (ADR-0057). Declaring `@targets(ex)` makes that promise
# explicit and the gate passes. Declaring `@targets(ex, rs, js)` here would be a
# compile error — the contract would name targets the FFI cannot reach.
@targets(ex)
mod Clock do
  pub def now() Int64 := :erlang.system_time()
end
